"""Grade 6 life essentials: forgiveness, honesty and gratitude in everyday life."""
from spec import L, U

M6 = U('Everyday goodness', 'Morality', 'How do I handle anger, tell the truth, and be thankful when it is not easy?', [
L('Sorry and forgiving: what to do with anger',
  'Conflicts at this age are loud: a friend betrays a secret, a sibling takes something, someone says a cruel thing. Anger is a normal feeling that needs a plan. This lesson puts together the Catechism on anger, the Our Father’s petition on forgiveness, reconciliation and the Sacrament of Penance, and gives students a concrete way to say sorry and forgive.',
  '2302-2306,2838-2845,1829,1422-1424,2447',
  'Matthew 5:23-24;Matthew 6:12-15;Matthew 18:21-22;Ephesians 4:26',
  'forgiveness mercy peace reconciliation',
  'What is the last thing someone did that made you really angry? What did you do with that feeling?',
  'Practise the “three-step sorry”: (1) say what you did, (2) say how it hurt the other person, (3) say what you will do to fix it. Then practise the “three-step forgiving”: (1) say what happened and how it felt, (2) choose not to get even, (3) pray for them. Role-play with a partner using a pretend problem.',
  'pater',
  id='6-L1',
  hook='Hold up a clenched fist. Ask: “What can you do with a clenched fist? Can you shake someone’s hand? Hold a gift?” Then slowly open it. Say, “Anger holds the fist closed. Forgiveness opens it.”',
  teach=[
    'Anger is a feeling and not a sin. Even Jesus got angry (Mark 3:5; John 2:15). The Catechism says that anger becomes sinful when it wants to harm someone or lasts as hatred (CCC 2302–2303). Paul says, “Be angry but do not sin; do not let the sun go down on your anger” (Ephesians 4:26). So: feel it, name it, and do something wise with it.',
    'Jesus gives a rule for relationships. “If you are offering your gift at the altar and there remember that your brother has something against you, leave your gift... first be reconciled” (Matthew 5:23–24). Notice that he says to go even when the other person has something against you. We go first. The Church says the same about the Mass: we offer a sign of peace to one another before Communion.',
    'Forgiving is not saying that what happened is okay, and it is not forgetting. It means deciding not to get even and wanting what is good for the other person (CCC 2842–2843). We pray it every day in the Our Father: “Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.” Jesus says if we do not forgive, we shut a door we also need (Matthew 6:14–15). Forgiving can take time, and it can start with a prayer for the person.',
    'When we have hurt someone, the sacrament of Penance is where God forgives us (CCC 1422–1424). But we should also say sorry to the person. A good sorry says what happened, does not make excuses, and offers to make it right. And if someone keeps hurting you or you feel unsafe, tell a trusted adult. Forgiving never means staying in danger.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is the difference between feeling angry and being cruel?',
    'What is a sorry that is not really a sorry? (“I am sorry if you were hurt.”)',
    'Why does Jesus say to go first, even when the other person is wrong?',
    'When is it important to tell an adult?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'Is it a sin to feel angry?|Always|No, the feeling is not a sin, but what we do with it can be|Only when you shout|Only for children|1|CCC 2302–2303.',
    'What are the three parts of a good sorry?|Excuse, blame, wait|Say what you did, say how it hurt, offer to fix it|Say nothing|Wait until they forgive you first|1|Reconciliation practice; Matthew 5:23–24.',
    'Does forgiving mean staying in danger?|Yes|No, forgiving is compatible with safety and telling an adult|Only if they say sorry|Only if they are family|1|CCC 2842–2843.',
  ],
  life='This week, find one person you need to say sorry to or forgive. Use the three-step plan. If you cannot talk to them, pray for them by name each night for a week. If the person has hurt you seriously, tell a trusted adult.',
  saint='Josephine Bakhita: a girl kidnapped and enslaved in Sudan who was set free and later forgave those who had wronged her.',
  misc=[
    '“Forgiving means it was okay.”',
    '“If I do not feel it, I have not forgiven.”',
    '“Saying sorry is a sign of weakness.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:confession'],
  mem='“Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.” (Our Father)',
  assess='Ask each student to say the three steps of a good sorry and the three steps of forgiving, and explain when to tell an adult.',
  script=dict(
    open='Hold up a tightly clenched fist. Ask, “What can you do with this hand? Can you shake hands? Hold a gift?” Slowly open it. Say, “Anger keeps the fist closed. Forgiveness opens it. Today we will practise opening.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “First: anger is a feeling. It is not a sin. Even Jesus got angry in the temple. The Catechism says anger becomes a sin when we want to hurt someone or when we hold on to it as hatred. St. Paul says, ‘Be angry but do not sin; don’t let the sun go down on your anger.’ So we feel it, name it, and choose what to do.”',
      'Say: “Jesus gives a rule: if you are at the altar and remember someone has something against you, go and make peace first. Notice he says to go even if you think they are wrong. At Mass we share a sign of peace before Communion. The Church says: make peace, then come to the altar.”',
      'Say: “Forgiving does not mean ‘it was fine.’ It does not mean forgetting. It means deciding not to get even and wanting what is good for the person. We say it in the Our Father every day. It may take time. You can start with a prayer for them. And if someone is hurting you, forgiving does not mean staying. Tell an adult.”',
      'Say: “When you hurt someone, say sorry to them and to God. In Confession God forgives us. To the person, a good sorry has three parts: say what you did, say how it hurt them, and say how you will fix it. No excuses. We will practise those now.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'When have you felt a fist inside? What helped it open?',
      'What is a fake sorry? What makes a sorry real?',
      'Who is one person you could forgive or say sorry to this week?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Write the three steps of a good sorry on a card.',
      'In pairs, role-play a pretend problem (a borrowed item that was broken). One says sorry using the three steps; the other practises forgiving.',
      'Swap roles. Then write the name of someone you will pray for this week.',
    ],
    close='Pray the Our Father slowly together, pausing at “as we forgive.” Say, “Open your hand. It is stronger than a fist.”'),
  tryit=['Learn the three-step sorry.', 'Role-play a sorry and a forgiving.', 'Write one person to pray for.'],
  saintStory='Bakhita was born around 1869 in Darfur, in what is now Sudan. When she was about seven, slave traders kidnapped her. The trauma was so great that she forgot her own name; the traders called her Bakhita, which means “lucky.” She was sold several times and was cruelly treated. She carried the scars for her whole life.\nIn Sudan she was eventually bought by an Italian consul, who took her to Italy as a nursemaid for a family. There she met the Canossian Sisters in Venice. For the first time she heard about a God who loved her. She said, “I was a slave and nobody had told me about God.” When a court in Venice ruled that she had been free since coming to Italy, she chose to stay with the sisters. She was baptized in 1890 and became a sister in 1896.\nShe spent more than forty years in a convent in Schio, where she was loved for her gentleness. When asked about her captors, she said that if she met them she would kneel and kiss their hands, because without them she would not have become a Christian. She died in 1947 and was canonized in 2000.',
),

L('Telling the truth when it costs you',
  'Students lie for small reasons: to avoid trouble, to look good, to protect a friend, to take a shortcut on homework. Cheating and small lies feel harmless and teach habits that last. The Church’s teaching on truthfulness, conscience and trust shows why honesty matters and how to recover when you slip.',
  '2464-2470,2475-2487,1776-1782,2409,1807',
  'Exodus 20:16;Proverbs 12:22;John 8:31-32;Luke 16:10',
  'conscience virtue justice',
  'When is it hardest to tell the truth, and what do you worry will happen?',
  'Write three short scenarios from school (a lost permission slip, a broken thing, a tempting answer on a test). For each, write the easy lie, the true answer and the first sentence you would use to say it. Role-play the true one with a partner.',
  'serenity',
  id='6-L2',
  hook='Tell the class a short story that is partly true and partly invented, then ask them to guess which parts are made up. Ask: “How easy was it to tell? What would happen if everyone did this all the time?”',
  teach=[
    'The eighth commandment, “You shall not bear false witness,” is about much more than the courtroom. The Catechism says it asks us to be truthful in what we say and do: “Truth or truthfulness is the virtue which consists in showing oneself true in deeds and truthful in words, and guarding against duplicity, dissimulation, and hypocrisy” (CCC 2468). That includes homework, tests, sports and online.',
    'Why does it matter? “Lying consists in speaking a falsehood with the intention of deceiving” (CCC 2482). The Catechism calls a lie “the most direct offense against the truth” and says that its seriousness depends on the harm it does (CCC 2483–2484). A small lie can be small; a lie that harms another person is not. But even small lies damage trust, and trust is the glue in friendship and in families.',
    'Conscience is the inner voice that tells us what is right. It must be trained by learning, prayer and advice, and it can be dulled (CCC 1776–1782). When you feel that little pinch before a lie, that is conscience. The more we listen, the clearer it gets. Honesty is a habit, and so is cheating.',
    'What if you already lied? Jesus says, “The truth will make you free” (John 8:32). The way back is simple: say it, say sorry, and make it right. The sacrament of Penance is there for exactly this (CCC 1422–1424). Many people find that telling the truth is scary for ten seconds and a relief for a long time. And honesty with small things prepares you to be honest with big ones: “One who is faithful in a very little is faithful also in much” (Luke 16:10).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Why do people lie? Which reason do you think is strongest?',
    'Is it ever okay to say less than everything? What is the difference between privacy and lying?',
    'Why does cheating hurt the person who cheats?',
    'What helps you tell the truth when you are scared?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What is a lie, according to the Catechism?|Any false statement|Speaking a falsehood with the intention of deceiving|Keeping a secret|A joke|1|CCC 2482.',
    'Which is the best way to recover from a lie?|Hope no one notices|Tell the truth, say sorry, and make it right|Tell another lie|Blame someone else|1|John 8:32; CCC 1422–1424.',
    'What is conscience?|A feeling of fear|The inner voice that judges right and wrong, which must be trained|The opinion of friends|A rule book|1|CCC 1776–1778.',
  ],
  life='For one week, catch yourself when you are tempted to lie, exaggerate or cheat, even slightly. When you notice, say the true thing instead. At the end of the week, tell a parent or teacher about one time it was hard.',
  saint='Dominic Savio: a boy who lived at Don Bosco’s school, known for his honesty and for standing up for classmates.',
  misc=[
    '“A little lie does not hurt anyone.”',
    '“Cheating is okay if everyone does it.”',
    '“If I am not caught, it does not count.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:aug-moral'],
  mem='“The truth will make you free.” (John 8:32)',
  assess='Ask each student to define a lie, name one time honesty is hard, and say the first sentence they would use to tell the truth.',
  script=dict(
    open='Tell a short story with three details, one of which you invented. Ask students to guess which one. Say, “If I told you stories like this every day, how would you know what to trust?” Let them answer.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The eighth commandment is ‘You shall not bear false witness.’ It started in a courtroom, but it reaches into homework, tests and group chats. The Catechism says truthfulness means being true in what we do as well as in what we say. A lie is speaking a falsehood in order to deceive. And cheating is a lie we act out.”',
      'Say: “Why does it matter? Because trust is like glue. Every lie, even a small one, makes the glue weaker. Soon people do not know whether to believe you. The Catechism says the seriousness of a lie depends on the harm. A small lie is not as bad as a big one, but small lies train us for bigger ones.”',
      'Say: “You have a conscience. It is the inner voice that says, ‘This is not right.’ It needs training: learning, prayer, advice from wise people. Listen to the pinch you feel before a lie. If you ignore it again and again, it gets quieter. If you listen, it gets clearer.”',
      'Say: “If you lied, there is a way back. Jesus says the truth will make you free. Say what is true, say sorry, and fix it. It is scary for ten seconds and a relief for a long time. And the sacrament of Penance is there to forgive and give strength. Being honest in small things prepares you for the big ones.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What do you think happens inside you when you lie?',
      'Who in your life do you trust most? Why?',
      'What is the first sentence you would use to tell a hard truth?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give out three scenario cards: a broken item, a lost permission slip, a tempting answer on a test.',
      'For each, write the easy lie, the true answer and your first sentence.',
      'Role-play the true answer with a partner.',
    ],
    close='Pray the serenity prayer together. Say, “Honesty is a muscle. Use it small, and use it today.”'),
  tryit=['Write the lie and the truth for three scenarios.', 'Write your first true sentence.', 'Role-play it with a partner.'],
  saintStory='Dominic Savio was born in 1842 in a small village in northern Italy, to a poor family. From a young age he loved to pray, and at seven he was allowed to receive First Communion early. At twelve he went to live at the Oratory in Turin, a home and school for poor boys run by a priest named John Bosco.\nDominic was an ordinary boy in many ways. He played games and joked with friends, but he had a firm rule: no cheating and no lying. A story is told that two boys once hid stones in their pockets to throw at a classmate in a quarrel. Dominic stepped between them, held up a crucifix, and asked them to say, “Can I hurt someone who has done nothing to me, if Jesus forgave those who crucified him?” They put the stones down.\nHe also set up a small group of boys who promised to help others and to be cheerful. He fell ill with a lung illness, and died on March 9, 1857, at 14. He was canonized in 1954, and is one of the youngest saints who was not a martyr.',
),

L('Thankful, enough, and sharing',
  'Children are surrounded by ads that say you need more. The Church answers with a quiet counter-message: gratitude is the root of happiness, “enough” is possible, and sharing is joy and not loss. The lesson ties together the Eucharist as thanksgiving, the seventh and tenth commandments, and the first Beatitude in terms an eleven-year-old can live.',
  '1328-1329,2443-2447,2544-2547,2828-2830,2638,1809',
  'Luke 17:11-19;Luke 12:15-21;Matthew 6:19-21;Acts 20:35',
  'gratitude stewardship almsgiving eucharist',
  'If you had to name five good things you have that you never earned, what would they be?',
  'Make a “thankful list” of ten things: three people, three things you use every day, two places, and two things you cannot buy. Then pick one thing you own and decide who could use it more, and give or lend it this week with permission.',
  'before-meals',
  id='6-L3',
  hook='Ask for two volunteers. Give one a big pile of coins or candy and the other nothing. Ask: “Is that fair? What would you want the first one to do?” Then read Acts 20:35: “It is more blessed to give than to receive.”',
  teach=[
    'Gratitude is at the center of Catholic worship. The word “Eucharist” means “thanksgiving” (CCC 1328–1329). At every Mass we say, “Let us give thanks to the Lord our God. It is right and just.” Saying thanks trains us to see gifts. In Luke 17, ten lepers are healed and only one returns to say thank you; Jesus notices (Luke 17:11–19).',
    'The tenth commandment is about greed and envy: wanting more and more, and being sad at what others have (CCC 2534–2540). Advertising is built to make us feel we do not have enough. The Church’s answer is “poverty of heart,” not having nothing but not being owned by things (CCC 2544–2547). Jesus tells a story of a farmer with full barns, called a fool, not because he had much but because he kept it all for himself (Luke 12:16–21).',
    'The seventh commandment teaches that the earth’s goods are for everyone, and that what we have is a gift to be shared (CCC 2402, 2443–2447). Giving does not have to be big. A child can share a snack, give time, make a card, or save part of an allowance. The Church calls this almsgiving, and it is one of the works of mercy.',
    'The Our Father asks only for “our daily bread” (CCC 2828–2830). That means enough for today, and for everyone, “our.” This is a prayer against hoarding and for sharing. When we trust that God gives what we need, we can open our hands. A grateful person tends to be a generous one.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is something you have that many people in the world do not?',
    'Is wanting more always wrong? How can you tell when wanting turns into greed?',
    'What is the best gift you ever gave? How did it feel?',
    'Why does the Our Father say “our” bread and not “my” bread?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does “Eucharist” mean?|Sacrifice|Thanksgiving|Feast|Meal|1|CCC 1328–1329.',
    'What did the farmer in Luke 12 do wrong?|He worked too hard|He kept everything for himself and forgot God|He built barns|He was too young|1|Luke 12:16–21.',
    'What is almsgiving?|Collecting things|Giving to the poor as a work of mercy|Throwing things away|Saving money|1|CCC 2447.',
  ],
  life='Every night this week, say three things you are thankful for before bed. Choose one thing to give or share this week (a toy, clothes, time, a snack) and do it. Ask an adult to help you pick where it will go.',
  saint='Martin of Tours: a Roman soldier who cut his cloak in half with his sword to share it with a freezing beggar.',
  misc=[
    '“Sharing means losing.”',
    '“Gratitude is only for holidays.”',
    '“Being poor in spirit means being poor.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:tithing-and-money'],
  mem='“It is more blessed to give than to receive.” (Acts 20:35)',
  assess='Ask each student to share three things they are thankful for, define “enough” in their own words, and name what they will give.',
  script=dict(
    open='Choose two volunteers. Give one a big pile of small items and the other nothing. Ask the class, “Is this fair? What would you want the first student to do?” After they answer, read Acts 20:35.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Gratitude is at the heart of Catholic worship. ‘Eucharist’ means thanksgiving. At every Mass the priest says, ‘Let us give thanks.’ Saying thanks trains our eyes to see gifts. In the story of the ten lepers, only one comes back to say thank you. Jesus notices.”',
      'Say: “Greed is wanting more and more, and envy is being sad when someone else has something. Ads try to make us feel we do not have enough. The Church answers with something surprising: poverty of heart. It does not mean having nothing; it means not being owned by things. The farmer in Luke 12 filled his barns and forgot God and everyone else.”',
      'Say: “The earth’s goods are a gift for everyone. What you have is something to share. Sharing can be small: a snack, time, a card, part of your allowance. The Church calls it almsgiving, and it is a work of mercy. Often it makes the giver happier than the receiver.”',
      'Say: “Look at the Our Father: ‘Give us this day our daily bread.’ Not my bread, our bread. And only for today. It is a prayer against hoarding. When we trust God to give what we need, we can open our hands. Grateful people usually turn out to be generous people.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What are three gifts you never earned?',
      'What would be “enough” for you? How do you know?',
      'What is one thing you could give away this week?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Write a thankful list of ten: three people, three daily things, two places, two things you cannot buy.',
      'Circle one thing you own that someone else could use more.',
      'Write who you will give it to and when. Ask an adult to help.',
    ],
    close='Pray the grace before meals together. Say, “Thanks first, then give. It changes how you see everything.”'),
  tryit=['Write a list of ten thanks.', 'Pick one thing to give.', 'Write who gets it and when.'],
  saintStory='Martin was born around 316 in what is now Hungary. His father was a Roman army officer, so Martin joined the army too, at fifteen, though he was drawn to Christianity as a boy. He was stationed in Gaul, now France.\nOne cold winter day around the year 335, at the gates of the city of Amiens, he met a beggar nearly frozen in the cold. Martin had nothing to give but his army cloak. He drew his sword, cut the cloak in two, and gave half to the man. That night, the story says, he dreamed of Christ wearing the half-cloak, saying to the angels, “Martin, still a catechumen, has clothed me.” He was baptized soon after.\nHe left the army, became a disciple of Hilary of Poitiers, and started a monastery. In 371 the people of Tours chose him as their bishop, though he was reluctant and is said to have hidden in a goose pen. He traveled around his diocese, set up rural parishes and cared for the poor. He died in 397. His feast is November 11, and his shared cloak became a symbol of charity.',
),
])
